  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A proactive Occupationnal Health and Safety interface at enterprise and ministerial levels established</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Seychelles has been working to improve workplace safety and health since 1978, with a law that has been updated over the years to keep pace with changing work environments and international standards. Despite this, the country has seen an increase in workplace accidents, including fatalities.

To address this, the government has committed to updating its occupational safety and health (OSH) policy and laws to better protect workers. The goal is to create a safe and healthy work environment for all, and to reduce the number of workplace accidents.

The current law, the OSH Decree, will be revised and updated to:

Improve workplace safety and health standards
Strengthen the roles and responsibilities of employers and employees
Encourage a preventative approach to safety and health
Improve enforcement and oversight of OSH practices

A comprehensive review of the current law will be conducted, involving government agencies, employers, workers, and safety experts. The review will assess the effectiveness of the current law and identify areas for improvement.

Based on the review, a new OSH decree will be drafted and adopted, with the aim of:

Strengthening the legal framework for OSH
Enhancing the roles and responsibilities of employers and employees
Promoting a preventative approach to safety and health
Improving enforcement mechanisms
By revising and adopting a new OSH decree, Seychelles aims to:

Improve workplace safety and health standards
Reduce the number of workplace accidents
Enhance stakeholder engagement and participation in promoting OSH compliance
Contribute to sustainable economic development
This initiative will help create a safer and healthier work environment for all workers in Seychelles, and support the country&apos;s economic growth and development.</narrative>
  </description>
